"""A Level Pure Mathematics with Mechanics (Upper Sixth): Statics of Rigid Bodies.

KKEVO draft content: standard A Level mechanics matched to the outline, not the Board's own text.
Slugs start with meu2-. Diagrams start with apomeu2-. g = 9.8 m/s^2 unless a question says 10.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("pure-mathematics-with-mechanics", "upper-sixth")

MOD = "Statics of Rigid Bodies"

LESSONS = []

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Moments
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MOD, topic="Moments",
    title="Moments and the principle of moments",
    slug="meu2-moments-and-principle-of-moments",
    minutes=22,
    summary="The moment of a force about a point is the force times the perpendicular distance; a body balanced on a pivot has equal clockwise and anticlockwise moments.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate the moment of a force about a point using the perpendicular distance",
        "Find the moment of a force acting at an angle to a rod",
        "State and use the principle of moments for a body balanced on a pivot",
        "Treat the weight of a uniform rod as acting at its midpoint",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Moment of a force", "Perpendicular distance", "Principle of moments", "Pivot", "Uniform rod"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A trader in Bafoussam lifts a heavy sack with a long plank pushed under it. A short push far from the support lifts a load that she could never lift by hand. The turning effect of a force is called its **moment**, and it depends on two things: how big the force is and how far from the turning point it acts."),
        T("In this lesson you learn how to measure that turning effect, and how to use it to find unknown forces and distances when a body is balanced. Throughout, $g = 9.8\\ \\text{m/s}^2$ unless a question says otherwise. We treat rods and planks as **rigid**: they do not bend."),
        H("The moment of a force"),
        D(r"The **moment** of a force about a point $O$ is the force multiplied by the **perpendicular distance** from $O$ to the line of action of the force. Its unit is the newton metre (N m).",
          title="Moment of a force", formula=r"\text{moment} = F \times d"),
        IMG("apomeu2-moment-force.svg", alt="A rod OP pivoted at O with a force F acting at P at an angle theta to the rod, and the perpendicular distance d from O to the line of the force",
            caption="Figure 1. The distance in a moment is the perpendicular distance d from O to the line of the force, not the length OP."),
        T(r"The **line of action** of a force is the straight line along which it pushes or pulls. If the force acts at a point $P$ at angle $\theta$ to the rod $OP$ of length $r$, the perpendicular distance is $d = r\sin\theta$. So the moment is $F r \sin\theta$."),
        KEY("Two ways to get the same moment", "Both methods always agree. Choose the one that is easier.", cards=[
            ["Perpendicular distance", r"Find $d$ from $O$ to the line of the force, then use $F \times d$."],
            ["Perpendicular component", r"Resolve $F$ into a part along the rod (no turning effect) and a part $F\sin\theta$ perpendicular to it, then use $F\sin\theta \times r$."],
        ]),
        D(r"A force whose line of action passes through the pivot has **zero** moment about it, because $d = 0$. This is why we often take moments about a point where an unknown force acts: that force disappears from the equation.",
          title="Zero moment"),
        H("Sense of a moment"),
        T("A moment turns a body either **clockwise** or **anticlockwise**. To add moments we need a sign convention. In this course we take **anticlockwise as positive** when we write one equation. Some textbooks use clockwise as positive: check your textbook. The answer does not change as long as you stay consistent."),
        H("The principle of moments"),
        D(r"If a body is in equilibrium (not turning), the sum of the clockwise moments about any point equals the sum of the anticlockwise moments about that point.",
          title="Principle of moments", formula=r"\text{sum of clockwise moments} = \text{sum of anticlockwise moments}"),
        T(r"The **weight of a uniform rod** acts at its **midpoint**. A plank 6 m long has its weight acting 3 m from either end. When every force on the body is a weight, the factor $g$ appears in every term and cancels, so you can work with masses directly. If a non-weight force (a push or a tension in newtons) is also present, you must convert masses to weights first."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a see-saw", intro="A uniform plank is pivoted at its midpoint. A child of mass 24 kg sits 2.0 m from the pivot. Where must a child of mass 30 kg sit on the other side so that the plank balances?", steps=[
            S("The plank's own weight acts at the pivot", text="So its moment about the pivot is zero. Only the two children matter."),
            S("Let the distance be $x$ m and take moments about the pivot", math=r"24g \times 2.0 = 30g \times x"),
            S("Cancel $g$ and solve", math=r"x = \frac{24 \times 2.0}{30} = 1.6"),
            S("Answer", text="The 30 kg child sits **1.6 m** from the pivot, on the opposite side. The heavier child sits closer."),
        ], check=r"24 \times 2.0 = 48 \quad\text{and}\quad 30 \times 1.6 = 48 \ \checkmark"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a force at an angle", intro="A spanner is 0.25 m long. A mechanic pulls at its end with a force of 120 N, at 30 degrees to the spanner. Find the moment about the bolt at the other end.", steps=[
            S("Perpendicular distance", math=r"d = 0.25\sin 30^\circ = 0.125\ \text{m}"),
            S("Moment", math=r"120 \times 0.125 = 15\ \text{N m}"),
            S("Second method: perpendicular component", math=r"120\sin 30^\circ = 60\ \text{N}, \qquad 60 \times 0.25 = 15\ \text{N m}", text="Both methods give **15 N m**."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using the length of the rod instead of the perpendicular distance", r"The Board's 2023 report notes that some candidates used the wrong quantity when finding a moment. A common slip is the wrong distance. If the force is not at right angles to the rod, the length of the rod is **not** the distance you need. Draw the line of the force and the perpendicular to it from the pivot, or resolve the force and use only the component perpendicular to the rod."),
        IMG("apomeu2-plank-pivot.svg", alt="A uniform plank AB of length 6 m on a pivot at C, 1.5 m from A, with loads of 60 kg at A, the plank's own weight at its midpoint and an unknown mass M kg at B",
            caption="Figure 2. The plank of Worked example 3. Distances are measured from the pivot C."),
        EX("Worked example 3: an off-centre pivot", intro="The uniform plank AB in Figure 2 has mass 30 kg and length 6 m. It rests on a pivot at C, 1.5 m from A. A load of 60 kg hangs from A and a sack of mass $M$ kg hangs from B. The plank is horizontal and in equilibrium. Find $M$ and the force the pivot exerts on the plank.", steps=[
            S("Distances from C", text="The weight of the plank acts at its midpoint, 3 m from A, so it is $3 - 1.5 = 1.5$ m from C on the side of B. The sack at B is $6 - 1.5 = 4.5$ m from C."),
            S("Clockwise moments about C (the plank and the sack)", math=r"30g \times 1.5 + Mg \times 4.5"),
            S("Anticlockwise moment about C (the load at A)", math=r"60g \times 1.5"),
            S("Principle of moments", math=r"45 + 4.5M = 90 \ \Rightarrow\ M = 10", text="The sack has mass **10 kg**."),
            S("Vertical equilibrium for the pivot force", math=r"R = (60 + 30 + 10)\times 9.8 = 980\ \text{N}", text="The pivot pushes up with **980 N**, which equals the total weight."),
        ]),
        TIP("Exam link: moments appear in Paper 1 (Section B, multiple choice on mechanics) and in Paper 3, where the Board's 2023 report names centre of mass and the use of moments among the topics tested. Always draw a clear force diagram with every distance measured from the pivot."),
        H("Quick checks"),
        QUIZ(r"A force of 40 N acts at a point $P$, 0.5 m from $O$. The line of the force makes an angle of $30^\circ$ with $OP$. What is its moment about $O$?",
             ["20 N m", "10 N m", "17.3 N m", "80 N m"], 1,
             r"The perpendicular distance is $0.5\sin 30^\circ = 0.25$ m, so the moment is $40 \times 0.25 = 10$ N m.",
             hint="Find the perpendicular distance from O to the line of the force first.",
             feedback=[r"This is $40 \times 0.5$, which ignores the angle. The distance must be perpendicular.", "",
                       r"This uses $\cos 30^\circ$. The perpendicular distance uses the sine of the angle between the force and the rod.",
                       "You divided the force by the distance. A moment is force times distance."]),
        QUIZ("A uniform rod is pivoted at one end O, with a force acting along a line that passes through O. What is the moment of that force about O?",
             ["The force times the length of the rod", "Half the force times the length", "Zero", "It cannot be found without the angle"], 2,
             "The perpendicular distance from O to a line that passes through O is zero, so the moment is zero.",
             hint="What is the perpendicular distance from a point to a line through that point?",
             feedback=["The rod's length is not the perpendicular distance to the line of the force.", "There is no reason to halve the force or the length.", "",
                       "The angle is not needed: the line goes through O, so $d = 0$."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("A boy of mass 40 kg sits 1.5 m from the pivot of a uniform see-saw. His sister has mass 30 kg. How far from the pivot must she sit, on the other side, for the see-saw to balance?",
           r"The plank's weight acts at the pivot. $40g \times 1.5 = 30g \times x$, so $x = \dfrac{60}{30} = 2.0$ m. The lighter child sits **2.0 m** from the pivot."),
        PQ(r"A door is 0.9 m wide. A force of 15 N is applied at the handle, at the edge, at an angle of $50^\circ$ to the door. Find the moment of the force about the hinge, in N m to 3 significant figures.",
           r"Perpendicular distance $= 0.9\sin 50^\circ = 0.6894$ m. Moment $= 15 \times 0.6894 = 10.3$ N m (3 s.f.). Check: $15\sin 50^\circ = 11.49$ N perpendicular to the door, and $11.49 \times 0.9 = 10.3$ N m."),
        PQ("A uniform beam of length 3 m and mass 12 kg rests horizontally on a pivot. A load of 8 kg hangs from one end A and a load of 2 kg from the other end B. Find the distance of the pivot from A.",
           r"Let the pivot be $x$ m from A. The 8 kg load is $x$ m left of the pivot. The beam's weight acts $1.5 - x$ m to the right, the 2 kg load $3 - x$ m to the right. Moments: $8x = 12(1.5 - x) + 2(3 - x)$. So $8x = 24 - 14x$, $22x = 24$ and $x = \dfrac{12}{11} = 1.09$ m (3 s.f.). The pivot is **1.09 m** from A."),
        PQ(r"A uniform rod OP of mass 5 kg and length 2.4 m is hinged at O and held at $30^\circ$ above the horizontal. Find the moment of the rod's weight about O.",
           r"The weight $5 \times 9.8 = 49$ N acts at the midpoint, 1.2 m along the rod. The perpendicular distance from O to the vertical line of the weight is the horizontal distance $1.2\cos 30^\circ = 1.039$ m. Moment $= 49 \times 1.039 = 50.9$ N m (3 s.f.), clockwise if the rod points to the right."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"The moment of a force about a point is $F \times d$, where $d$ is the **perpendicular** distance to the line of the force. It is measured in N m.",
            r"For a force at angle $\theta$ to a rod of length $r$, the moment is $F r\sin\theta$.",
            "A force through the pivot has zero moment: take moments about the point with most unknown forces.",
            "In equilibrium, clockwise moments equal anticlockwise moments about any point.",
            "The weight of a uniform rod acts at its midpoint; masses can be used instead of weights only if every force is a weight."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Equilibrium of rigid bodies
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MOD, topic="Equilibrium of rigid bodies",
    title="Equilibrium of a rod or beam under coplanar forces",
    slug="meu2-equilibrium-of-rods-and-beams",
    minutes=24,
    summary="A rod or beam in equilibrium has zero resultant force in two directions and zero total moment; resolve forces, take moments about a clever point and solve for the reactions.",
    objectives=[
        "State the conditions for a rigid body to be in equilibrium under coplanar forces",
        "Find the reactions at two supports of a horizontal beam",
        "Find the tension in a string and the force at a hinge holding a rod",
        "Decide when a beam is about to tilt, using a zero reaction",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Equilibrium", "Reaction", "Tension", "Hinge", "About to tilt", "Coplanar forces"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A plank across a stream near Kumba carries a farmer and her sacks of cocoa. Each bank pushes up on the plank. How much does each bank push? The answer decides whether the plank holds, and whether it tips when she walks to one end."),
        T(r"In the last lesson you used moments for a plank on one pivot. Now the plank is supported in two places, or hinged at a wall and held by a string. We need more than one equation, so we combine **resolving forces** with **taking moments**. All forces here lie in one plane: they are **coplanar**. We use $g = 9.8\ \text{m/s}^2$."),
        H("The conditions for equilibrium"),
        D(r"A rigid body acted on by coplanar forces is in **equilibrium** (it does not move or turn) if, and only if, three conditions hold: the forces resolved in one direction sum to zero, the forces resolved in a perpendicular direction sum to zero, and the total moment about any point is zero.",
          title="Equilibrium of a rigid body",
          formula=r"\sum F_x = 0, \qquad \sum F_y = 0, \qquad \sum \text{moments about any point} = 0"),
        T("The first two conditions stop the body sliding; the third stops it turning. A moment equation about a second point can replace a resolving equation, and this is often the quickest way to find an unknown force."),
        H("What force does each connection give?"),
        KEY("Forces to draw on every diagram", "Draw all forces, label each one, and use the direction rules below.", cards=[
            ["Weight", "Acts vertically downwards at the centre of the body. A uniform rod: at the midpoint."],
            ["Support or smooth surface", "The reaction acts at right angles to the surface."],
            ["String or cable", "The tension pulls along the string, away from the body."],
            ["Hinge or pin joint", r"The force can have any direction, so draw two components, usually horizontal $H$ and vertical $V$."],
            ["Rough surface", "A normal reaction plus a friction force along the surface (see the lesson on toppling and sliding)."],
        ]),
        KEY("A routine that works", "Follow the same steps in every question.", cards=[
            ["Step 1", "Draw a large diagram with every force, every angle and every distance."],
            ["Step 2", "Take moments about a point where an unknown force acts. That force then has zero moment."],
            ["Step 3", "Resolve horizontally and vertically to find the remaining unknowns."],
            ["Step 4", "Check with a moment equation about a different point."],
        ]),
        IMG("apomeu2-beam-supports.svg", alt="A horizontal uniform beam AB of length 6 m resting on supports at C and D, 1 m from each end, with its own weight at the midpoint and a load of 80 kg at 4 m from A",
            caption="Figure 1. The beam of Worked example 1. R_C and R_D are the upward reactions at the supports."),
        EX("Worked example 1: reactions at two supports", intro="The uniform beam AB in Figure 1 has mass 40 kg and length 6 m. It rests on supports at C and D, where AC = 1 m and DB = 1 m. A man of mass 80 kg stands on the beam 4 m from A. Find the reactions at C and D.", steps=[
            S("Moments about C removes $R_C$", text="The beam's weight is 2 m from C (its midpoint is 3 m from A). The man is $4 - 1 = 3$ m from C. D is $5 - 1 = 4$ m from C."),
            S("Equation", math=r"R_D \times 4 = 40g \times 2 + 80g \times 3 = 320g"),
            S("Solve", math=r"R_D = 80g = 784\ \text{N}"),
            S("Resolve vertically", math=r"R_C + R_D = (40 + 80)g = 1176 \ \Rightarrow\ R_C = 392\ \text{N}"),
        ], check=r"\text{Moments about D: } R_C \times 4 = 40g \times 2 + 80g \times 1 = 160g \Rightarrow R_C = 40g = 392\ \checkmark"),
        H("When does a beam tilt?"),
        D(r"A beam on two supports is **about to tilt** about one support when the reaction at the other support has just become zero. At that moment the beam is still in equilibrium, so the same equations apply with that reaction set equal to zero.",
          title="About to tilt"),
        EX("Worked example 2: how far can a porter walk?", intro="The beam of Worked example 1 (mass 40 kg, length 6 m, supports at 1 m and 5 m from A) now carries a porter of mass 100 kg who walks from the middle towards the end B. How far from A is he when the beam is about to tilt?", steps=[
            S("Which way will it tilt?", text="The porter walks beyond D, so the beam tilts about D and the reaction at C becomes zero."),
            S("Moments about D with $R_C = 0$", text="Let the porter be $x$ m from A. His distance beyond D is $x - 5$. The weight of the beam is $5 - 3 = 2$ m on the other side of D."),
            S("Equation", math=r"100g(x - 5) = 40g \times 2"),
            S("Solve", math=r"x - 5 = 0.8 \ \Rightarrow\ x = 5.8"),
            S("Answer", text="The beam is about to tilt when the porter is **5.8 m** from A, which is 0.2 m from the end B."),
        ]),
        IMG("apomeu2-rod-hinge-string.svg", alt="A uniform rod AB 2 m long hinged at A to a vertical wall, with a string from B to a point C on the wall above A making 40 degrees with the rod, and weights of 6 kg at the midpoint and 4 kg at B",
            caption="Figure 2. The rod of Worked example 3: hinged at A and held horizontal by the string BC."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a rod held by a string", intro=r"In Figure 2 the uniform rod AB has mass 6 kg and length 2 m. It is hinged at A to a vertical wall and held horizontal by a string BC, where C is on the wall above A and the angle ABC is $40^\circ$. A load of mass 4 kg hangs from B. Find the tension in the string and the magnitude and direction of the force of the hinge on the rod.", steps=[
            S("Moments about A removes the hinge force", text=r"The string's vertical component at B is $T\sin 40^\circ$ and its distance from A is 2 m. The rod's weight is 1 m from A, the load 2 m."),
            S("Equation", math=r"T\sin 40^\circ \times 2 = 6g \times 1 + 4g \times 2 = 14g"),
            S("Solve for $T$", math=r"T = \frac{7g}{\sin 40^\circ} = 106.7\ \text{N}", text="The tension is **106.7 N** (4 s.f.)."),
            S("Resolve horizontally", math=r"H = T\cos 40^\circ = 81.75\ \text{N}", text="The string pulls B towards the wall, so the hinge pushes the rod away from the wall."),
            S("Resolve vertically", math=r"V + T\sin 40^\circ = 10g \ \Rightarrow\ V = 98 - 68.6 = 29.4\ \text{N upwards}"),
            S("Magnitude and direction of the hinge force", math=r"R = \sqrt{81.75^2 + 29.4^2} = 86.9\ \text{N}, \qquad \tan\alpha = \frac{29.4}{81.75} \Rightarrow \alpha = 19.8{}^\circ", text=r"The hinge force is **86.9 N**, at $19.8^\circ$ above the horizontal and away from the wall."),
        ], check=r"\text{Moments about B: } V \times 2 = 6g \times 1 = 58.8 \Rightarrow V = 29.4 \ \checkmark"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the second component of the hinge force", r"A hinge can push in any direction. If you draw only a vertical reaction at the hinge, your horizontal equation will not balance. Always draw $H$ and $V$ at a hinge, then find them from the two resolving equations."),
        MISTAKE("Using the whole tension in the moment equation", r"The moment of a string tension about a point on the rod uses the **perpendicular** component of $T$, here $T\sin 40^\circ$. Writing $T \times 2$ treats the string as if it were at right angles to the rod. Resolve the tension first, or find its perpendicular distance."),
        TIP("Exam link: equilibrium of rods and beams, tilting and hinge forces are standard Paper 3 mechanics and are suitable for Paper 1 (Section B) when only a reaction or a tension is asked for. Start with the moment equation that contains the fewest unknowns."),
        H("Quick checks"),
        QUIZ("A uniform beam rests on two supports. As a load is moved towards one end, the reaction at the nearer support increases and the reaction at the far support:",
             ["Increases", "Stays the same", "Decreases and can reach zero when the beam is about to tilt", "Becomes negative"], 2,
             "The total reaction is fixed by the weights, so as one reaction grows the other falls. At the point of tilting it is zero. A support can only push, so a reaction cannot be negative.",
             hint="The two reactions together must always equal the total weight.",
             feedback=["The total of the two reactions is fixed, so they cannot both increase.", "Moving the load changes the moments, so the reactions change.", "",
                       "A support can push but not pull. At the limit the reaction is zero and then the beam tilts."]),
        QUIZ("A rod is hinged at O, held by a string, and carries a load. To find the tension in the string using one equation, you should take moments about:",
             ["The point where the load hangs", "The hinge O", "The midpoint of the rod", "The point where the string meets the wall"], 1,
             "The hinge force acts at O, so it has no moment about O. The equation contains only the tension and the known weights.",
             hint="Choose the point where an unknown force acts.",
             feedback=["The hinge force would appear in the equation, giving two unknowns.", "", "Neither the hinge force nor the tension acts there, so both would appear.",
                       "The tension acts at that point on the string's line, so its own moment would vanish and it could not be found."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("A uniform plank AB of mass 20 kg and length 4 m rests on supports at its ends A and B. A man of mass 60 kg stands 1 m from A. Find the reactions at A and B.",
           r"Moments about A: $R_B \times 4 = 60g \times 1 + 20g \times 2 = 100g$, so $R_B = 25g = 245$ N. Vertically: $R_A + R_B = 80g = 784$, so $R_A = 784 - 245 = 539$ N. Check by moments about B: $R_A \times 4 = 60g \times 3 + 20g \times 2 = 220g$, so $R_A = 55g = 539$ N."),
        PQ("A uniform beam AB of mass 30 kg and length 5 m rests on supports at A and at C, where CB = 1 m. A sack of mass $m$ kg is placed at B. Find the greatest value of $m$ for which the beam stays in equilibrium on both supports.",
           r"The beam is about to tilt about C when $R_A = 0$. The beam's weight acts 2.5 m from A, so $4 - 2.5 = 1.5$ m from C. The sack is 1 m from C on the other side. Moments about C: $mg \times 1 = 30g \times 1.5$, so $m = 45$. The greatest mass is **45 kg**."),
        PQ(r"A uniform rod AB of mass 5 kg and length 2 m is hinged at A and held horizontal by a string BC, where C is on the wall above A and angle $ABC = 30^\circ$. Find the tension $T$ and the magnitude of the hinge force.",
           r"Moments about A: $T\sin 30^\circ \times 2 = 5g \times 1$, so $T = 49$ N. Horizontal: $H = T\cos 30^\circ = 42.4$ N. Vertical: $V = 49 - 49\sin 30^\circ = 24.5$ N. The hinge force is $\sqrt{42.4^2 + 24.5^2} = 49.0$ N, at $30^\circ$ above the horizontal. It equals the tension here only because the rod carries no extra load."),
        PQ("A uniform beam AB of mass 50 kg and length 8 m rests on supports at A and D, where AD = 6 m. A load of mass 40 kg is hung from the end B. Find the reaction at each support.",
           r"Moments about A: $R_D \times 6 = 50g \times 4 + 40g \times 8 = 520g$, so $R_D = 86.67g = 849$ N (3 s.f.). Vertically: $R_A = 90g - 849.3 = 882 - 849.3 = 32.7$ N. The reaction at A is small because the load at the end is nearly enough to tilt the beam about D."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"A rigid body is in equilibrium when $\sum F_x = 0$, $\sum F_y = 0$ and the total moment about any point is zero.",
            "Draw every force: weights at the centre, reactions at right angles to smooth surfaces, tension along strings, two components at a hinge.",
            "Take moments about a point where an unknown force acts, so that force drops out of the equation.",
            "The moment of a tension uses its perpendicular component. Check an answer with moments about a second point.",
            "A beam on two supports is about to tilt when one reaction is zero."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Centre of mass (1): particles and simple uniform bodies
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MOD, topic="Centre of mass",
    title="Centre of mass of particles and simple uniform bodies",
    slug="meu2-centre-of-mass-particles-and-uniform-bodies",
    minutes=22,
    summary="The centre of mass of a system of particles is the mass-weighted average of their positions; symmetry and standard results give the centre of simple uniform bodies.",
    objectives=[
        "Define the centre of mass and find it for particles on a line and in a plane",
        "Use symmetry to locate the centre of mass of uniform rods, rectangles and circles",
        "Quote and use the standard results for a triangle and a semicircular lamina",
        "Combine a rod and particles by treating each part as a mass at its own centre",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Centre of mass", "Moment about an axis", "Uniform body", "Symmetry", "Lamina"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A lorry on the Douala to Yaounde road carries sacks of maize. If the sacks are stacked far to one side, the lorry leans and may tip on a bend. Everything depends on one point: the **centre of mass**, the point where the whole weight of a body seems to act."),
        T(r"In this lesson you find that point for a few particles, and for simple uniform shapes. In the next lesson you will use it for composite shapes and shapes with holes."),
        H("Definition and the key formula"),
        D(r"The **centre of mass** of a body is the point through which its weight acts, whatever the orientation of the body. A body hung from its centre of mass balances in any position. Near the surface of the Earth, where $g$ is the same at every point, the **centre of gravity** is at the same point, so at this level the two names mean the same thing.",
          title="Centre of mass"),
        T(r"For particles of masses $m_1, m_2, \ldots$ at distances $x_1, x_2, \ldots$ from a line, the weight of the whole system must have the same moment about that line as the weights of the parts. That fact gives the formula."),
        D(r"For particles of masses $m_i$ at positions $x_i$ along a line, the centre of mass is at $\bar{x}$. In a plane, the same formula is used for the $y$ coordinates.",
          title="Centre of mass of particles",
          formula=r"\bar{x} = \frac{\sum m_i x_i}{\sum m_i}, \qquad \bar{y} = \frac{\sum m_i y_i}{\sum m_i}"),
        EX("Worked example 1: three particles on a rod", intro="Particles of mass 2 kg, 3 kg and 5 kg are fixed to a light rod, at distances 0 m, 4 m and 10 m from one end A. Find the distance of their centre of mass from A.", steps=[
            S("Total mass", math=r"2 + 3 + 5 = 10\ \text{kg}"),
            S("Sum of moments about A", math=r"2 \times 0 + 3 \times 4 + 5 \times 10 = 62\ \text{kg m}"),
            S("Divide", math=r"\bar{x} = \frac{62}{10} = 6.2\ \text{m}", text="The centre of mass is **6.2 m** from A, close to the heaviest particle."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: particles in a plane", intro="Particles of mass 1, 2, 3 and 4 kg are placed at the corners $(0, 0)$, $(3, 0)$, $(3, 4)$ and $(0, 4)$ of a rectangle (lengths in metres). Find the coordinates of their centre of mass.", steps=[
            S("Total mass", math=r"1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10"),
            S("$x$ coordinate", math=r"\bar{x} = \frac{1(0) + 2(3) + 3(3) + 4(0)}{10} = \frac{15}{10} = 1.5"),
            S("$y$ coordinate", math=r"\bar{y} = \frac{1(0) + 2(0) + 3(4) + 4(4)}{10} = \frac{28}{10} = 2.8", text="The centre of mass is at **(1.5 m, 2.8 m)**."),
        ]),
        H("Uniform bodies and symmetry"),
        D(r"A body is **uniform** if its mass is spread evenly: every equal volume has equal mass. A **lamina** is a flat body so thin that it can be treated as a plane shape. For a uniform lamina, the mass of a piece is proportional to its area.",
          title="Uniform body and lamina"),
        D(r"If a uniform body has a line (or plane) of symmetry, its centre of mass lies on it. If it has two lines of symmetry, the centre of mass is where they cross.",
          title="Symmetry rule"),
        T("By this rule the centre of mass of a uniform rod is its midpoint. For a rectangle it is where the diagonals meet. For a circle it is the centre. For other shapes we use standard results."),
        IMG("apomeu2-centroids-standard.svg", alt="A triangle with its centre of mass G one third of the height above the base, and a semicircle with G a distance 4r over 3 pi above the diameter",
            caption="Figure 1. Two standard results: the triangle and the semicircular lamina."),
        CODE("""Uniform shape                  Distance of the centre of mass
rod                            midpoint
rectangle, parallelogram       where the diagonals cross
circular disc                  centre
triangular lamina              h/3 from the base (h = perpendicular height)
                               (where the three medians meet)
semicircular lamina, radius r  4r/(3 pi) from the diameter
sector, radius r, angle 2a     2r sin(a)/(3a) from the centre (a in radians)
solid hemisphere, radius r     3r/8 from the flat face
solid cone, height h           h/4 from the base""", "text", title="Standard results (check your textbook or formula list)",
             caption="The semicircle, sector, hemisphere and cone results are normally quoted. Learn the triangle by its derivation."),
        EX("Worked example 3: why a triangle's centre of mass is h/3 above its base", intro="Show that the centre of mass of a uniform triangular lamina of base $b$ and height $h$ is $h/3$ above the base.", steps=[
            S("Cut into thin strips parallel to the base", text=r"At height $y$ above the base the strip has width $b\left(1 - \frac{y}{h}\right)$ and thickness $dy$, so its area, and so its mass, is proportional to $\left(1 - \frac{y}{h}\right)dy$."),
            S("Moment of all the strips", math=r"\int_0^h y\left(1 - \frac{y}{h}\right)dy = \frac{h^2}{2} - \frac{h^2}{3} = \frac{h^2}{6}"),
            S("Total mass of the strips", math=r"\int_0^h \left(1 - \frac{y}{h}\right)dy = h - \frac{h}{2} = \frac{h}{2}"),
            S("Divide", math=r"\bar{y} = \frac{h^2/6}{h/2} = \frac{h}{3}", text="So the centre of mass is **$h/3$ above the base**. The same is true for each of the three sides, which is why it is where the medians meet."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 4: a rod and a particle", intro="A uniform rod AB of mass 3 kg and length 2 m has a particle of mass 1 kg fixed at B. Find the distance of the centre of mass of the combined body from A.", steps=[
            S("Treat the rod as a particle of mass 3 kg at its midpoint", text="Its midpoint is 1 m from A. The particle is 2 m from A."),
            S("Use the formula", math=r"\bar{x} = \frac{3(1) + 1(2)}{3 + 1} = \frac{5}{4} = 1.25"),
            S("Answer", text="The centre of mass is **1.25 m** from A."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using different origins for different particles", r"Measure every position from the same point. If one distance is from the left end and another from the right end, the formula no longer works. Write the origin on your diagram."),
        MISTAKE("Putting the centre of a semicircle halfway", r"The centre of mass of a semicircular lamina is $\dfrac{4r}{3\pi} \approx 0.424r$ from the diameter, not $\dfrac{r}{2}$. Because there is less area near the curved edge than near the diameter, the centre is closer to the diameter than the middle."),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's 2023 report lists the centre of mass of a plane lamina among the Paper 3 topics. Centre-of-mass questions can also appear in Paper 1 (Section B, multiple choice). Show every distance from one origin, and give the answer with its units."),
        H("Quick checks"),
        QUIZ("A uniform lamina is a rectangle with a circular hole cut out of its exact centre. Where is the centre of mass of the remaining lamina?",
             ["Nearer the edge than the centre", "At the centre of the rectangle", "On the edge of the hole", "It cannot be found"], 1,
             "The shape still has two lines of symmetry, through the centre of the rectangle, so the centre of mass is there even though no material is at that point.",
             hint="Apply the symmetry rule.",
             feedback=["The shape is symmetrical, so there is no reason for the centre to move.", "",
                       "The edge of the hole is a circle, not a single point, and the hole is symmetrical about the rectangle's centre.", "Symmetry places it exactly."]),
        QUIZ("Particles of mass 2 kg and 6 kg are 4 m apart. How far from the 2 kg particle is the centre of mass?",
             ["1 m", "2 m", "3 m", "4 m"], 2,
             r"Measure from the 2 kg particle: $\bar{x} = \dfrac{2(0) + 6(4)}{8} = 3$ m. The centre of mass is nearer the heavier particle.",
             hint="Place the 2 kg particle at the origin and the 6 kg particle at 4 m.",
             feedback=["This is the distance from the heavier particle, 1 m, not from the lighter one.", "That would be the midpoint, which is only right if the masses are equal.", "", "That is the position of the 6 kg particle."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Particles of mass 2 kg, 5 kg and 3 kg are placed at 1 m, 4 m and 9 m from the end A of a light rod. Find the distance of their centre of mass from A.",
           r"$\bar{x} = \dfrac{2(1) + 5(4) + 3(9)}{10} = \dfrac{49}{10} = 4.9$ m."),
        PQ("Particles of mass 3 kg, 2 kg and 5 kg are at the points $(2, 1)$, $(-1, 4)$ and $(0, -2)$. Find the coordinates of their centre of mass.",
           r"Total mass 10 kg. $\bar{x} = \dfrac{3(2) + 2(-1) + 5(0)}{10} = 0.4$. $\bar{y} = \dfrac{3(1) + 2(4) + 5(-2)}{10} = 0.1$. The centre of mass is at $(0.4, 0.1)$."),
        PQ("A uniform rod AB of mass 2 kg and length 1.2 m has a particle of mass $m$ kg fixed at B. The centre of mass of the combined body is 0.8 m from A. Find $m$.",
           r"$\dfrac{2(0.6) + m(1.2)}{2 + m} = 0.8$, so $1.2 + 1.2m = 1.6 + 0.8m$, giving $0.4m = 0.4$ and $m = 1$. The particle has mass **1 kg**."),
        PQ("A triangular lamina has vertices $(1, 1)$, $(7, 2)$ and $(4, 9)$. Find the coordinates of its centre of mass.",
           r"The centre of mass of a uniform triangle is at the mean of its vertices: $\left(\dfrac{1 + 7 + 4}{3}, \dfrac{1 + 2 + 9}{3}\right) = (4, 4)$."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"The centre of mass of particles is $\bar{x} = \sum m x / \sum m$ and $\bar{y} = \sum m y / \sum m$, measured from one origin.",
            "A uniform body's centre of mass lies on any line of symmetry: rod midpoint, rectangle diagonals, circle centre.",
            r"A triangular lamina has its centre of mass $h/3$ from the base; a semicircular lamina $4r/3\pi$ from the diameter.",
            "For a uniform lamina, mass is proportional to area. A rod or part of a body can be treated as a particle at its own centre.",
            "Near the Earth's surface, the centre of mass and the centre of gravity are the same point."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Centre of mass (2): composite laminas
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MOD, topic="Centre of mass",
    title="Composite laminas, holes and laminas hanging freely",
    slug="meu2-centre-of-mass-composite-laminas",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Split a composite lamina into simple parts, treat each part as an area at its own centre, subtract a hole as a negative area, and use the result to find how the lamina hangs.",
    objectives=[
        "Find the centre of mass of a composite lamina by dividing it into rectangles, triangles and semicircles",
        "Treat a hole as a negative area",
        "Present the working in a table of areas and moments",
        "Find the angle at which a lamina hangs when suspended from a point",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Composite lamina", "Area as mass", "Hole as negative mass", "Suspension", "Table of moments"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A school workshop in Bamenda cuts steel plates into L-shapes to make brackets. To hang one on a wall, or to know where it balances on a finger, you need the centre of mass of the L. The L is not a standard shape, but it is made of two rectangles, and each rectangle is easy."),
        T("This is the main method for laminas: **divide, locate, combine**. It is the method the Board's 2023 report names under centre of mass of a plane lamina."),
        H("The method"),
        D(r"A uniform lamina is divided into parts with areas $A_1, A_2, \ldots$ whose centres of mass are at $(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \ldots$. Because mass is proportional to area, the centre of mass of the whole lamina is at the point below.",
          title="Composite lamina", formula=r"\bar{x} = \frac{\sum A_i x_i}{\sum A_i}, \qquad \bar{y} = \frac{\sum A_i y_i}{\sum A_i}"),
        KEY("Five steps", "Use the same routine each time.", cards=[
            ["1. Axes", "Put axes on the shape with the origin at a corner, so that every coordinate is positive."],
            ["2. Divide", "Split into rectangles, triangles, semicircles or circles. A hole is a part with negative area."],
            ["3. Table", "List each part's area and the coordinates of its centre."],
            ["4. Moments", "Work out area times $x$ and area times $y$ for each part, then add."],
            ["5. Divide and check", "Divide the totals by the total area. Check with any symmetry."],
        ]),
        IMG("apomeu2-lamina-L.svg", alt="An L-shaped lamina 4 cm wide at the bottom and 8 cm tall, split into a 4 by 2 rectangle and a 2 by 6 rectangle, with the centre of mass of each part and of the whole shown",
            caption="Figure 1. The L-shaped lamina of Worked example 1, split into two rectangles."),
        CODE("""Part          Area   x of G   y of G   Area*x   Area*y
Rectangle 1      8      2        1       16        8
Rectangle 2     12      1        5       12       60
Whole           20    x-bar    y-bar     28       68""", "text", title="Table for Worked example 1 (origin at the bottom left corner, lengths in cm)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: an L-shaped lamina", intro="A uniform lamina is L-shaped (Figure 1): the bottom arm is 4 cm wide and 2 cm high, the vertical arm is 2 cm wide and the whole lamina is 8 cm tall. Find the distances of its centre of mass from the left edge and from the bottom edge.", steps=[
            S("Divide", text=r"Rectangle 1 is the bottom arm, $4 \times 2$ cm. Rectangle 2 is the part above it, $2 \times 6$ cm. The table above lists their areas and centres."),
            S("Totals from the table", math=r"\sum A = 20, \quad \sum Ax = 16 + 12 = 28, \quad \sum Ay = 8 + 60 = 68"),
            S("Divide by the total area", math=r"\bar{x} = \frac{28}{20} = 1.4, \qquad \bar{y} = \frac{68}{20} = 3.4"),
            S("Answer", text="The centre of mass is **1.4 cm from the left edge and 3.4 cm from the bottom edge**. It lies inside the lamina, nearer the tall thin arm than the base, because the vertical arm has more area."),
        ]),
        H("Holes: negative area"),
        D(r"To find the centre of mass of a lamina with a hole, take the full shape (without the hole) and **subtract** the hole. In the table the hole has **negative** area, and its centre is the centre of the hole.",
          title="Holes", note="Equivalent to adding a part of negative mass. The total area is the area of the full shape minus the area of the hole."),
        IMG("apomeu2-lamina-hole.svg", alt="A rectangular lamina 40 cm by 30 cm with a circular hole of radius 5 cm whose centre is 10 cm from the left edge and 15 cm from the bottom edge",
            caption="Figure 2. The lamina of Worked example 2, with a circular hole of radius 5 cm."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a rectangle with a circular hole", intro="A uniform rectangular lamina is 40 cm by 30 cm. A circular hole of radius 5 cm is cut so that its centre is 10 cm from the left edge and 15 cm from the bottom edge (Figure 2). Find the distance of the centre of mass of the remaining lamina from the left edge.", steps=[
            S("Parts", text="The full rectangle has area $1200$ cm$^2$ and its centre is 20 cm from the left edge. The hole has area $\\pi \\times 5^2 = 25\\pi = 78.54$ cm$^2$ and its centre is 10 cm from the left edge."),
            S("Moments about the left edge", math=r"1200 \times 20 - 25\pi \times 10 = 24000 - 785.4 = 23214.6"),
            S("Remaining area", math=r"1200 - 25\pi = 1121.46"),
            S("Divide", math=r"\bar{x} = \frac{23214.6}{1121.46} = 20.7\ \text{cm}", text="The centre of mass is **20.7 cm** from the left edge (3 s.f.). By symmetry it is 15 cm from the bottom edge. It has moved to the right, away from the hole, as it should."),
        ], check=r"\text{With no hole } \bar{x} = 20. \text{ Removing material on the left moves the centre to the right: } 20.7 > 20 \ \checkmark"),
        H("Laminas hanging freely"),
        D(r"When a lamina is suspended freely from a point, it hangs in equilibrium with its centre of mass **vertically below** the point of suspension. This is because the weight acts at the centre of mass: any other position gives a turning moment about the point of suspension.",
          title="Hanging in equilibrium"),
        EX("Worked example 3: the angle a lamina hangs at", intro="The lamina of Worked example 2 is freely suspended from the bottom left corner O. Find the angle that the bottom edge (the 40 cm edge) makes with the vertical.", steps=[
            S("Centre of mass is vertically below O", text="The line from O to G is vertical. G is at $(20.7, 15)$ relative to O along and up the edges."),
            S("The angle between OG and the bottom edge", math=r"\tan\phi = \frac{15}{20.7} \ \Rightarrow\ \phi = 35.9{}^\circ"),
            S("Interpret", text="The line OG is vertical and makes $35.9^\\circ$ with the bottom edge. So the **bottom edge makes an angle of $35.9^\\circ$ with the vertical**."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Adding the hole instead of subtracting", r"The hole removes material, so its area and its moment are subtracted. If you add them, you get a centre of mass that moves **towards** the hole instead of away from it. Always check the direction of the shift."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that mass is proportional to area only for a uniform lamina", "If the lamina is made of two different materials, or has different thickness, you must use the masses of the parts, not their areas."),
        TIP("Exam link: this is the form of the plane-lamina questions in Paper 3. The Board's 2023 report names the centre of mass of a plane lamina as a topic. A table with area, $x$, $y$, area times $x$ and area times $y$ makes your method clear and easy to mark. Give distances from a named edge."),
        H("Quick checks"),
        QUIZ("A uniform circular disc has a small circular hole near its edge. The centre of mass of the remaining lamina is:",
             ["At the centre of the disc", "On the line from the centre through the hole, on the far side of the centre from the hole", "On the same side as the hole", "At the centre of the hole"], 1,
             "Removing material from one side moves the centre of mass away from that side, along the line of symmetry through the centre of the hole.",
             hint="Which way does the centre move when you take material away?",
             feedback=["The hole breaks the symmetry, so the centre moves.", "", "Material has been removed there, so the centre moves away from it, not towards it.", "There is no material at the hole, so the centre of mass cannot be there."]),
        QUIZ("Which expression gives the $x$ coordinate of the centre of mass of a lamina made of two parts with areas $A_1$ and $A_2$ and centres at $x_1$ and $x_2$?",
             [r"$\dfrac{x_1 + x_2}{2}$", r"$\dfrac{A_1 x_1 + A_2 x_2}{A_1 + A_2}$", r"$A_1 x_1 + A_2 x_2$", r"$\dfrac{A_1 + A_2}{x_1 + x_2}$"], 1,
             "The area-weighted average of the centres: each centre counts in proportion to its area.",
             hint="Larger parts count for more.",
             feedback=["This ignores the areas: it is only right if the two areas are equal.", "", "This is the total moment. It must be divided by the total area.", "This is not a weighted average of positions."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("A uniform lamina is a rectangle 20 cm by 12 cm with a semicircle of radius 6 cm attached to one of the 12 cm sides, so that the diameter of the semicircle is that side. Find the distance of the centre of mass from the opposite 12 cm side.",
           r"Measure from the far 12 cm side. Rectangle: area $240$, centre 10 cm away. Semicircle: area $\frac{1}{2}\pi \times 36 = 18\pi = 56.55$; its centre is $\frac{4 \times 6}{3\pi} = 2.546$ cm beyond the diameter, so $20 + 2.546 = 22.546$ cm away. $\bar{x} = \dfrac{240(10) + 56.55(22.546)}{240 + 56.55} = \dfrac{3675.0}{296.55} = 12.4$ cm (3 s.f.). By symmetry the centre is on the axis through the middle of the 12 cm sides."),
        PQ(r"A uniform square lamina has side $2a$. A square of side $a$ is cut from one corner O, so that, with O as the origin and axes along the sides, the lamina occupies $0 \leq x, y \leq 2a$ except $0 \leq x, y \leq a$. Find the coordinates of its centre of mass.",
           r"Full square: area $4a^2$, centre $(a, a)$. Removed square: area $a^2$, centre $\left(\frac{a}{2}, \frac{a}{2}\right)$. $\bar{x} = \dfrac{4a^2(a) - a^2\left(\frac{a}{2}\right)}{3a^2} = \dfrac{3.5a^3}{3a^2} = \dfrac{7a}{6}$. By symmetry $\bar{y} = \dfrac{7a}{6}$ too, so the centre of mass is at $\left(\frac{7a}{6}, \frac{7a}{6}\right)$."),
        PQ("A uniform rectangular lamina is 30 cm by 20 cm. A circular hole of radius 4 cm has its centre 8 cm from one of the 20 cm sides and 10 cm from each 30 cm side. Find the distance of the centre of mass of the remaining lamina from that 20 cm side.",
           r"Full rectangle: area $600$, centre 15 cm from the side. Hole: area $16\pi = 50.27$, centre 8 cm from it. $\bar{x} = \dfrac{600(15) - 50.27(8)}{600 - 50.27} = \dfrac{9000 - 402.1}{549.73} = 15.6$ cm (3 s.f.)."),
        PQ("ABC is a uniform triangular lamina, right-angled at B, with AB = 12 cm and BC = 9 cm. It is freely suspended from A. Find the angle that AB makes with the vertical.",
           r"Put B at the origin, A at $(0, 12)$ and C at $(9, 0)$. G is at $(3, 4)$. The line from A to G is vertical. The vector from A to G is $(3, -8)$, so the angle between AG and AB (which goes straight down) satisfies $\tan\phi = \dfrac{3}{8}$, so $\phi = 20.6^\circ$."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"For a composite lamina, $\bar{x} = \sum A x / \sum A$ and $\bar{y} = \sum A y / \sum A$, using the area of each part and the coordinates of its centre.",
            "Set out a table: part, area, $x$, $y$, area times $x$, area times $y$. Use one origin.",
            "A hole is a part with negative area: its moment is subtracted, and the centre of mass moves away from the hole.",
            "Mass is proportional to area only if the lamina is uniform and of constant thickness.",
            "A freely suspended lamina hangs with its centre of mass vertically below the point of suspension, which gives the angle at which it hangs."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Toppling and sliding
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MOD, topic="Toppling and sliding",
    title="Toppling and sliding of blocks and ladders",
    slug="meu2-toppling-and-sliding",
    minutes=25,
    summary="A body on a rough surface slides when the friction needed exceeds the limit mu R, and topples when its weight acts outside the base; compare the two to find which happens first.",
    objectives=[
        "State the conditions for sliding and for toppling of a block on a horizontal surface",
        "Find the force that makes a block slide or topple, and decide which happens first",
        "Find the angle of a rough slope at which a block slides or topples",
        "Find whether a ladder against a smooth wall rests in equilibrium, and the least angle it can make",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Sliding", "Toppling", "Limiting friction", "Coefficient of friction", "Line of action of the weight", "Ladder"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A lorry loaded with tall cartons takes a bend near Mbouda. It may skid sideways, or it may lean over and tip. Both are about forces on a rigid body, but they are different. Sliding is about friction. Toppling is about moments."),
        H("Sliding and toppling"),
        D(r"Friction can supply any force up to a limit. The body is **in equilibrium** if the friction needed, $F$, satisfies $F \leq \mu R$. It is **about to slide** (limiting equilibrium) when $F = \mu R$, and it **slides** if the friction needed is more than $\mu R$.",
          title="Sliding", formula=r"F \leq \mu R, \qquad \text{limiting: } F = \mu R"),
        D(r"A body is **about to topple** (or tip) about an edge when the reaction of the surface acts at that edge. At that moment the line of action of the weight, and of the resultant of all the other forces, passes through the edge. If the weight acts outside the base, the body topples.",
          title="Toppling"),
        KEY("Two separate checks", "A body can fail in either way. Do both checks and find which happens first.", cards=[
            ["Check for sliding", r"Resolve horizontally and vertically to find $F$ and $R$, then compare $F$ with $\mu R$."],
            ["Check for toppling", r"Take moments about the edge about which the body would turn. At the point of toppling, $R$ and $F$ both act at that edge, so they have no moment."],
            ["Which first?", "As the pushing force increases, the first limit reached is the one that happens."],
        ]),
        IMG("apomeu2-toppling-block.svg", alt="A rectangular crate pushed by a horizontal force P at a height of 1 m, about to tip about its lower right edge B, with the weight W acting at the centre 0.6 m from B and the reaction R and friction F at B",
            caption="Figure 1. The crate of Worked example 1, about to topple about the edge B."),
        EX("Worked example 1: slide or topple?", intro="A uniform crate of mass 50 kg stands on a rough horizontal floor. It is 1.2 m wide and 1.6 m high. A horizontal force $P$ is applied to one side at a height of 1.0 m above the floor (Figure 1), and increased slowly from zero. Find the value of $P$ at which the crate (a) slides, (b) topples about the edge B. Which happens first if the coefficient of friction is (i) 0.4, (ii) 0.7?", steps=[
            S("Weight and normal reaction", math=r"W = 50 \times 9.8 = 490\ \text{N}, \qquad R = 490\ \text{N}", text="Vertical equilibrium gives $R = W$."),
            S("(a) Sliding", math=r"P = F = \mu R = 490\mu", text="So the crate slides when $P = 490\\mu$: for $\\mu = 0.4$ this is 196 N, for $\\mu = 0.7$ it is 343 N."),
            S("(b) Toppling about B", text="When it is about to topple, $R$ and $F$ act at B. Moments about B: the force $P$ turns the crate one way and the weight, 0.6 m from B, the other way."),
            S("Equation", math=r"P \times 1.0 = 490 \times 0.6 \ \Rightarrow\ P = 294\ \text{N}", text="This does not depend on $\\mu$."),
            S("(i) $\\mu = 0.4$", text="Sliding needs 196 N and toppling needs 294 N. The smaller force is reached first, so the crate **slides** (at 196 N)."),
            S("(ii) $\\mu = 0.7$", text="Sliding would need 343 N, but toppling needs only 294 N. The crate **topples** (at 294 N)."),
            S("The dividing value of $\\mu$", math=r"490\mu = 294 \Rightarrow \mu = 0.6", text="If $\\mu < 0.6$ the crate slides first; if $\\mu > 0.6$ it topples first."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using F = mu R when the body is not about to slip", r"The formula $F = \mu R$ applies only in **limiting** equilibrium. When the body is not about to move, $F$ takes whatever value balances the other forces. Find $F$ from the equilibrium equations, then check that $F \leq \mu R$."),
        MISTAKE("Taking moments about the wrong edge", "A body pushed to the right tips about its **lower right** edge, the edge on the side the force pushes towards. Moments about the other edge give nonsense. Draw the picture first and mark the edge B about which it turns."),
        H("Blocks on a rough slope"),
        D(r"A uniform rectangular block rests on a rough slope inclined at $\theta$ to the horizontal. The base along the slope has length $b$, the height perpendicular to the slope is $h$, and the centre of mass G is at the middle of the block. **Sliding.** Resolving along and perpendicular to the slope: $F = W\sin\theta$ and $R = W\cos\theta$. The block slides when $F > \mu R$, that is when $W\sin\theta > \mu W\cos\theta$, so when $\tan\theta > \mu$.",
          title="Sliding on a slope", formula=r"\text{slides when } \tan\theta > \mu"),
        D(r"**Toppling.** The block is about to topple about the lower edge when the vertical line through G passes through that edge. G is $\frac{b}{2}$ along the slope and $\frac{h}{2}$ above it, so $\tan\theta = \dfrac{b/2}{h/2}$.",
          title="Toppling on a slope", formula=r"\text{topples when } \tan\theta > \frac{b}{h}"),
        IMG("apomeu2-block-slope.svg", alt="A rectangular block on a slope at angle theta with its weight acting vertically down from the centre G through the lower pivot edge of the base",
            caption="Figure 2. At the point of toppling, the vertical through G passes through the lower edge of the base."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a block on a tilting ramp", intro="A uniform rectangular block has a base 0.4 m long along the slope and a height of 0.9 m. It rests on a rough slope that is slowly tilted. Find the angle at which the block starts to slide or topple if the coefficient of friction is (a) 0.5, (b) 0.4.", steps=[
            S("Toppling angle", math=r"\tan\theta = \frac{b}{h} = \frac{0.4}{0.9} = 0.444 \ \Rightarrow\ \theta = 24.0{}^\circ", text="This does not depend on $\\mu$."),
            S("(a) $\\mu = 0.5$", math=r"\tan\theta = 0.5 \ \Rightarrow\ \theta = 26.6{}^\circ \ \text{(sliding)}", text="As the slope increases from zero, toppling at $24.0^\\circ$ comes first. The block **topples at $24.0^\\circ$**."),
            S("(b) $\\mu = 0.4$", math=r"\tan\theta = 0.4 \ \Rightarrow\ \theta = 21.8{}^\circ \ \text{(sliding)}", text="Sliding at $21.8^\\circ$ comes before toppling at $24.0^\\circ$. The block **slides at $21.8^\\circ$**."),
        ]),
        H("Ladders"),
        T("A ladder against a wall is a classic sliding problem. We take a **uniform ladder** with its foot A on rough horizontal ground and its top B against a **smooth** wall. The wall can only push at right angles to itself, so it gives a horizontal reaction $S$. The ground gives a normal reaction $R$ upwards and a friction force $F$ towards the wall."),
        IMG("apomeu2-ladder.svg", alt="A uniform ladder AB 5 m long leaning at 60 degrees against a smooth vertical wall with its foot on rough ground, showing the weight W, the normal reaction R and friction F at A and the horizontal reaction S at B",
            caption="Figure 3. The forces on the ladder of Worked example 3."),
        EX("Worked example 3: will the ladder slip?", intro="A uniform ladder AB of mass 20 kg and length 5 m rests with A on rough horizontal ground, where the coefficient of friction is 0.4, and B against a smooth vertical wall. The ladder is at $60^\\circ$ to the horizontal (Figure 3). (a) Show that the ladder is in equilibrium. (b) Find the least angle with the horizontal at which the ladder can rest.", steps=[
            S("Resolve vertically and horizontally", math=r"R = W = 20 \times 9.8 = 196\ \text{N}, \qquad F = S"),
            S("Moments about A removes $R$ and $F$", math=r"S \times 5\sin 60^\circ = W \times 2.5\cos 60^\circ"),
            S("Solve for $S$", math=r"S = \frac{196 \times 2.5 \times 0.5}{5 \times 0.866} = 56.6\ \text{N}", text="So the friction needed is $F = 56.6$ N."),
            S("(a) Compare with the limit", math=r"\mu R = 0.4 \times 196 = 78.4\ \text{N}", text="$56.6 < 78.4$, so the friction needed is less than the most that is available. The ladder **stays in equilibrium**."),
            S("(b) General angle $\\theta$", math=r"S = \frac{W\cos\theta}{2\sin\theta} = \frac{W}{2\tan\theta}, \qquad F = S \leq \mu R = \mu W \ \Rightarrow\ \tan\theta \geq \frac{1}{2\mu}"),
            S("Substitute $\\mu = 0.4$", math=r"\tan\theta \geq 1.25 \ \Rightarrow\ \theta \geq 51.3{}^\circ", text="The least angle is **51.3 degrees**."),
        ], check=r"\text{At } 60^\circ: \ \tan 60^\circ = 1.73 \geq 1.25 \ \checkmark"),
        TIP("Exam link: sliding, toppling and ladder problems are Paper 3 mechanics, built on friction and moments. The multiple-choice paper (Paper 1, Section B) often asks only for the force or the angle at which the limit is reached. Always state which edge you take moments about and whether the equilibrium is limiting."),
        H("Quick checks"),
        QUIZ("A tall narrow wardrobe stands on a rough floor and is pushed by a horizontal force at its top. Compared with a short wide chest of the same material, it is:",
             ["Less likely to topple, because it is heavier", "More likely to topple, because the weight is closer to the edge and the force acts higher", "Equally likely to topple", "Unable to slide"], 1,
             "To topple, the pushing force must have a larger moment than the weight about the lower edge. A tall narrow body has a short distance from the weight to the edge and a long distance from the force, so toppling needs less force.",
             hint="Compare the two moments about the lower edge.",
             feedback=["Weight helps to prevent toppling, but so does width, and the tall wardrobe has a narrow base.", "", "The shape matters: the moment arm of the weight is the half-width, the moment arm of the force is the height.",
                       "It can slide if the push exceeds $\\mu R$, so this is incorrect."]),
        QUIZ("A block rests on a slope with $\\mu = 0.6$. The slope is increased until the block topples before it slides. The block's base along the slope is $b$ and its height is $h$. What must be true?",
             [r"$b/h > 0.6$", r"$b/h < 0.6$", r"$b/h = 0.6$ exactly, and the block does both at once", "It depends only on the mass"], 1,
             r"Toppling occurs at $\tan\theta = b/h$ and sliding at $\tan\theta = 0.6$. Toppling is reached first when $b/h < 0.6$.",
             hint="The smaller of the two angles is reached first.",
             feedback=["If $b/h > 0.6$, sliding would be reached first.", "", "That is the dividing case: both would happen together.", "Neither angle depends on the mass."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("A uniform solid cube of side 0.4 m and mass 30 kg rests on a rough horizontal floor with $\\mu = 0.5$. A horizontal force $P$ acts on a vertical face at a height of 0.3 m above the floor. Find the value of $P$ at which the cube (a) slides, (b) topples. Which happens as $P$ increases from zero?",
           r"$W = 30 \times 9.8 = 294$ N. (a) Sliding: $P = \mu R = 0.5 \times 294 = 147$ N. (b) Toppling: moments about the lower edge, the weight is 0.2 m from it: $P \times 0.3 = 294 \times 0.2$, so $P = 196$ N. The smaller value is reached first, so the cube **slides at 147 N** and does not topple."),
        PQ("A uniform rectangular block has a base 0.3 m long along the slope and a height of 0.5 m. It rests on a rough slope with $\\mu = 0.8$. The slope is slowly tilted. Does the block slide or topple, and at what angle?",
           r"Toppling: $\tan\theta = \dfrac{0.3}{0.5} = 0.6$, so $\theta = 31.0^\circ$. Sliding: $\tan\theta = 0.8$, so $\theta = 38.7^\circ$. Toppling is reached first: the block **topples at $31.0^\circ$**."),
        PQ("A uniform ladder AB of mass 20 kg and length 5 m rests with A on rough horizontal ground ($\\mu = 0.4$) and B against a smooth vertical wall, at $60^\\circ$ to the horizontal. A man of mass 60 kg climbs the ladder. How far along the ladder from A can he climb before it is about to slip?",
           r"Let him be $x$ m from A. Vertically: $R = 80 \times 9.8 = 784$ N. Limiting friction $F = 0.4 \times 784 = 313.6$ N $= S$. Moments about A: $S \times 5\sin 60^\circ = 20g \times 2.5\cos 60^\circ + 60g \times x\cos 60^\circ$. So $313.6 \times 4.330 = 245 + 294x$, giving $1357.9 - 245 = 294x$ and $x = 3.79$ m (3 s.f.). He can climb **3.79 m** along the ladder."),
        PQ("A uniform rectangular block of mass 40 kg is 0.6 m wide and 1.2 m high. It stands on a rough floor with $\\mu = 0.8$. A rope fixed to the top edge is pulled horizontally with tension $T$. Show that the block topples before it slides, and find the value of $T$ at which it topples.",
           r"$W = 392$ N. Sliding would need $T = \mu W = 313.6$ N. Toppling about the lower far edge: $T \times 1.2 = 392 \times 0.3$, so $T = 98$ N. Since $98 < 313.6$, the block topples first, at **$T = 98$ N**. (Toppling occurs first whenever $\mu > 0.25$.)"),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"A body does not slide while the friction needed is at most $\mu R$; in limiting equilibrium $F = \mu R$.",
            "A body is about to topple when the reaction acts at the edge: take moments about that edge, so $R$ and $F$ have no moment.",
            r"On a slope, a block slides when $\tan\theta > \mu$ and topples when $\tan\theta > b/h$; whichever angle is smaller comes first.",
            r"For a ladder on rough ground against a smooth wall, $F = S = \dfrac{W}{2\tan\theta}$ and the ladder rests only if $\tan\theta \geq \dfrac{1}{2\mu}$.",
            "Always check both sliding and toppling, and say which happens first."),
    ],
))
